WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
The report, and the decision it supports.

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:17.837
Put the results in one table: for each system, the test-set size, parse rate, exact match with its interval, per-field accuracy, cost per 1,000 tasks with its assumptions, and p50 and p95 latency.

3
00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:30.832
Below it, the paired differences against the frontier model, the error-category table, and two sentences on what the benchmark doesn’t cover (traffic unlike the test set, drift over time, adversarial inputs).

4
00:00:31.112 --> 00:00:43.293
Then write the decision the way a reviewer can check it: “On 150 frozen cases, the fine-tuned model’s exact match is within 2 points below to 12 points above the frontier model’s.

5
00:00:43.573 --> 00:00:57.077
At our expected volume and 20 percent GPU utilization it costs about 30 percent less per 1,000 tasks, and its p95 latency is under a second against over two seconds.

6
00:00:57.357 --> 00:01:08.173
We recommend switching the batch pipeline, keeping the frontier model as a fallback for snippets the small model fails to parse, and re-running this benchmark monthly on a fresh sample.

7
00:01:08.453 --> 00:01:10.523
” That paragraph is the deliverable.

8
00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:13.256
The rest of the report is the evidence for it.
